.TH CSTR_SUBSTRING 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cstr_substring \- create a new string from a sub-range; calls fatal_err() on failure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cstring.h>

cstr result = cstr_substring(s, start, len);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
GNU C statement expression that calls cstring_substring() and calls
fatal_err() if the result is NULL (allocation failure). Returns the new
cstring as a value so it can be used directly in expressions.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s      Source cstr
start  Zero-based start index
len    Number of characters to include
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
New cstr containing the requested sub-range
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This macro uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}) and requires GCC or
Clang
The range is clamped at the string end rather than rejected
Caller must destroy the returned cstring when done
Use cstring_substring() directly if you need to handle errors gracefully
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cstr_construct(src, "Hello, world!");
cstr sub = cstr_substring(src, 7, 5);   /* "world" */
printf("%s\en", cstr_c_str(sub));
cstr_destroy(sub);
cstr_destroy(src);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstring_substring (3),
.BR cstr_copy (3),
.BR cstr_split (3)
